# Composite Sensor — Python_subprocess_Abuse

**Architecture:** 1 — Composite Sensor  
**Author:** Ala Dabat | MTDF 2026  
**MITRE ATT&CK:** T1059.006 — Python  
**Platform:** MDE Advanced Hunting — Linux + Windows  
**Lifecycle:** Production-Candidate (ADX validation pending)  
**Base Score:** 55 · **Threshold:** ≥ 75  
**Anchoring:** Intent-First  

---

## Purpose

High-confidence production alert for Python web application RCE via subprocess abuse. Fires when a Python WSGI/ASGI worker (`gunicorn`, `uwsgi`, `uvicorn`) or a web-serving Python process spawns a shell, downloader, or interpreter with RCE-indicative command-line content.

---

## Why Composite and Not Router Rule

Python web server processes have a **single noise domain**: WSGI/ASGI worker processes serving HTTP requests.

`gunicorn` spawns Python worker processes — not bash shells. `uvicorn` handles async requests — not curl downloads. The suppression model is consistent: data science environments (Jupyter, Anaconda) receive a soft penalty because Jupyter kernels legitimately spawn subprocesses.

**Single noise domain → composite.**

> **Important caveat:** If your environment has heavy Jupyter/notebook usage on the same hosts as production web servers, consider adding a Router Rule to separate the two Python contexts. The `-25` soft penalty may not be sufficient in high-volume notebook environments.

---

## Attack Vectors Covered

| Attack Type | Framework | Example Sink |
|-------------|-----------|-------------|
| Command injection | Flask/Django | `os.system(request.args.get('cmd'))` |
| SSTI (Server-Side Template Injection) | Jinja2 | `{{config.__class__.__init__.__globals__['os'].popen('bash')}}` |
| Pickle deserialisation | Any | `pickle.loads(user_controlled_data)` |
| Python eval/exec injection | Any | `eval(user_input)` |
| Subprocess with user input | Flask/Django | `subprocess.call(user_controlled_string, shell=True)` |

---

## Unique Signal — `HasSubprocessArg`

Python-specific signal: when the initiating Python process command line contains subprocess invocation patterns (`os.system(`, `subprocess.call(`, `subprocess.Popen(`), this is a direct code-level indicator of the exploit sink.

```
HasSubprocessArg — Python subprocess sink in parent cmdline
Scores: +20
Triggers on: os.system(, subprocess.call(, subprocess.run(, __import__('os')
```

This is particularly valuable for detecting SSTI and eval injection where the parent Python process command line contains the injected payload.

---

## Scoring

| Signal | Score | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| Base (minimum truth) | +55 | gunicorn/uwsgi → shell = structural truth |
| Reverse shell pattern | +30 | `/dev/tcp/` = near-certain C2 |
| Windows PowerShell cradle | +30 | IEX from Python = Windows RCE |
| Pipe-to-shell | +25 | Download-and-execute chain |
| Remote URL | +20 | External staging |
| Subprocess sink in parent | +20 | Python-specific code sink indicator |
| Base64/encoded | +15 | Obfuscation |
| Data science context | -25 | Jupyter/Anaconda = legitimate subprocess |

---

## When It Fires

- `gunicorn` spawns `bash` with `/dev/tcp/` → **CRITICAL (85)**
- `uvicorn` spawns `curl` with remote URL → **MEDIUM (75)**
- `python3` spawns `bash` with `subprocess.call(` in parent cmdline → **HIGH (80)**

---

## Framework-Specific Investigation

| Framework | Vulnerable Pattern | Where to Look |
|-----------|-------------------|---------------|
| Flask | `os.system(request.args['cmd'])` | Route handlers with OS calls |
| Django | `subprocess.call(request.POST['cmd'], shell=True)` | View functions |
| FastAPI | SSTI in Jinja2 templates | Template files |
| Any | `pickle.loads(user_data)` | Deserialisation endpoints |

